rows.solution:
(a) (i) Petals: they are brightly coloured and attract insects for pollination.

(ii) Anther: it produces the pollen grains (male gametes).

(iii) Style: it provides the path through which the pollen tube grows to reach the ovary.

(iv) Ovary: it contains the ovules, each of which has an egg cell (female gamete).

(b) Unisexual flower: papaya (or watermelon). Bisexual flower: hibiscus (or mustard).

After fertilisation:

the zygote divides several times to form an embryo within the ovule;

the ovule develops a tough coat and changes into a seed;

the ovary grows rapidly and ripens to form a fruit;

the petals, sepals, stamens and style shrivel and fall off.
